# Intellectual Property & Collaboration Principles

## Purpose

This document outlines the intellectual property principles that guide collaborations involving Stewardship Performance Index (SPI), TEEHC (The Ecological Equivalent of Hyperscale Computing), and related stewardship frameworks developed by Misfit Genius.

Its purpose is to establish a clear understanding of ownership while encouraging open collaboration with municipalities, academic institutions, industry partners, governments, and community organizations.

These principles are intended as a discussion document and do not replace a formal legal agreement.

---

# Guiding Philosophy

Innovation is strengthened through collaboration.

Meaningful progress often requires expertise from many disciplines, including engineering, ecology, planning, infrastructure, public policy, economics, and community engagement.

The intention of these principles is not to restrict collaboration, but to ensure that all parties understand the distinction between existing intellectual property and work developed together during a project.

---

# Existing Intellectual Property

Prior to any collaboration, Misfit Genius retains ownership of all pre-existing intellectual property, including but not limited to:

* The Stewardship Performance Index (SPI)
* TEEHC (The Ecological Equivalent of Hyperscale Computing)
* Associated methodologies
* Assessment frameworks
* Scoring models
* Stewardship principles
* Documentation
* Research
* Concepts
* Diagrams
* Visual identity
* Branding
* Logos
* Trademarks
* Original written material

Unless otherwise agreed in writing, discussions regarding these concepts do not transfer ownership of the underlying intellectual property.

---

# Collaborative Development

Future collaborations may produce new ideas, methodologies, technical solutions, or project-specific implementations.

Ownership of work developed during a collaboration should be clearly defined through a written agreement before substantial work begins.

Possible arrangements may include:

* Project-specific licences
* Joint ownership of newly developed work
* Independent ownership of project deliverables
* Shared commercialization agreements
* Other mutually agreed arrangements

The appropriate model should reflect the nature of the collaboration and the contributions of each participant.

---

# Pilot Projects

Pilot projects provide an opportunity to evaluate stewardship frameworks within real-world environments.

Participation in a pilot does not imply ownership of the underlying framework.

Organizations participating in a pilot may receive permission to implement, evaluate, or contribute to the framework under terms established through a separate agreement.

---

# Transparency

All parties should have a clear understanding of:

* Existing intellectual property
* Newly developed intellectual property
* Rights of use
* Publication rights
* Attribution
* Confidential information
* Commercialization opportunities

Whenever possible, these expectations should be documented before collaborative work begins.

---

# Stewardship Through Partnership

The long-term vision for SPI and TEEHC is collaborative.

The challenges surrounding artificial intelligence, infrastructure, environmental stewardship, and community resilience cannot be addressed by a single organization.

Collaboration with municipalities, Indigenous communities, governments, industry, researchers, and non-profit organizations is encouraged.

The objective is to build a framework that benefits everyone while respecting the contributions and intellectual property of every participant.

---

# Future Agreements

This document represents the collaboration principles of Misfit Genius.

Formal collaborations may require additional documents, including:

* Non-Disclosure Agreements (NDAs)
* Memoranda of Understanding (MOUs)
* Collaboration Agreements
* Research Agreements
* Pilot Agreements
* Intellectual Property Licensing Agreements

Any legally binding rights or obligations will be established through those agreements.

